KGC: ODRL policy pairs over published vocabularies
==================================================

A TPTP export of the odrl-grounded-benchmark,
https://github.com/Daham-Mustaf/odrl-grounded-benchmark, tag v1.2. The
names are provisional: KGC is a proposed domain code and the problems are
numbered in sequence. The original name of each problem is in its % Names
field and in manifest.csv. SPC, Rating and Syntax are left to TPTP.

What the problems are
---------------------

The 111 problems come from 60 pairs of ODRL policies (Open Digital Rights
Language). Each pair constrains one property of a use, such as its
purpose, its place, its language or its file type, with values from a
published vocabulary: the Data Privacy Vocabulary, its Locations
extension, GeoNames, the EU file type table or the IANA Language Subtag
Registry. The question is whether some use can meet both policies.

A problem holds the vocabulary's published statements, read as a partial
order (Axioms/KGC000+0.ax and one file per vocabulary); the declarations
the parties make, that two values are distinct or that nothing lies below
both, written in the problem; and one condition: that some use meets the
two policies, or that no use does. Equality is built in and no axioms of
equality are added. An inequation is written only where a declaration
states it: two names are not assumed to name two things. Every problem is
function-free FOF without a conjecture, so its status is Satisfiable or
Unsatisfiable.

How a verdict follows
---------------------

Each pair of policies has two checks: one states that some use meets the
two policies, the other that no use does.

    first Unsatisfiable      Incompatible
    second Unsatisfiable     Compatible
    both Satisfiable         Unknown

Both Unsatisfiable would mean the statements contradict each other; it
does not occur. A Satisfiable problem also shows that the statements are
consistent, so there is no separate consistency problem. The % Comments of
each problem name its partner and the pair's verdict.

Merged checks
-------------

The 60 pairs have 120 checks, and 111 problems. A problem is exported
once: two checks whose formulas and include lines are the same after
renaming the case are one problem, and % Names lists both. 8 problems are
such groups:

    KGC063+1   KGC362-1 KGC365-1
    KGC064+1   KGC362-2 KGC365-2
    KGC079+1   KGC375-1 KGC379-1
    KGC080+1   KGC375-2 KGC379-2
    KGC083+1   KGC377-1 KGC378-2 KGC398-2
    KGC085+1   KGC378-1 KGC398-1
    KGC088+1   KGC382-1 KGC391-1
    KGC089+1   KGC382-2 KGC391-2

Versions
--------

Version 1 (+1), 111 problems: 71 Satisfiable, 40 Unsatisfiable. These are
the queries of the repository. Where a constraint fixes the value of the
use, the condition tries only the concepts that value allows; elsewhere it
lists every concept of the vocabulary. The declaration instances are those
among the concepts the condition names and the concepts that lie between
two of them, which gives the same status as all of them (the repository's
tree_expand.py says why).

Version 2 (+2), 86 problems: 57 Satisfiable, 29 Unsatisfiable. The
condition lists every concept of the vocabulary in every part, as the
paper's definition states, with the declaration instances among all
concepts of the vocabulary. The versions have the same status. A
disjunction or conjunction of more than 64 members is written in balanced
groups of at most 64; it is the same formula, nested about log n deep, so
that a recursive parser does not run out of stack on the ten thousand
disjuncts of the largest.

Version 3 (+3), 2 problems: 1 Satisfiable, 1 Unsatisfiable. The checks of
a merged problem can differ over the whole vocabulary. Each distinct full
form is then its own version, and % Names says which check it is:

    KGC088+2   KGC382-1
    KGC088+3   KGC391-1
    KGC089+2   KGC382-2
    KGC089+3   KGC391-2

A full form is not written for 30 checks: 10 under the ISO rule, whose
declarations have 12,382,776 pairs over the vocabulary, and 20 whose
condition and declarations come out the same over the whole vocabulary, so
that it would repeat version 1.

    KGC023     KGC317-1   the same formulas as version 1
    KGC024     KGC317-2   the same formulas as version 1
    KGC033     KGC322-1   the same formulas as version 1
    KGC034     KGC322-2   the same formulas as version 1
    KGC041     KGC334-1   declarations of 12,382,776 pairs (the ISO rule)
